-- | The LCP anchor overlay contract.
--
-- Carries an ATR hash alongside a Canton settlement that has no room for one. The atrHash is stored as
-- 64-character LOWERCASE HEX WITHOUT the `0x` prefix — the form `atrHashToLedgerText` produces and the
-- form a participant `/v1/query` filters on. The TypeScript codec normalizes to and from LCP's canonical
-- `0x`-prefixed spelling at the package boundary, so the on-ledger value has exactly one shape.
--
-- STAKEHOLDERS ARE THE TWO TRANSACTING PARTIES AND NOBODY ELSE. `buyer` is the signatory (the party whose
-- authority creates the contract) and `seller` is an observer. Daml contract visibility is limited to
-- stakeholders, which is precisely why the binding manifest declares `zeroPartyRecoverable: false`: a
-- neutral verifier holding only a settlement reference sees nothing here until one of them grants access.
-- Adding a third observer would widen that, and would be a change to the binding's declared recovery
-- properties rather than a detail — do not add one without moving the manifest with it.
--
-- The template is deliberately minimal. It is an ANCHOR, not an agreement: it asserts that these two
-- parties associated this record with this transaction, and nothing about the terms themselves, which live
-- in the ATR the hash commits to. Anything more would put contract logic where the terms document belongs.
module Main where

template LcpAnchor
  with
    buyer : Party
      -- ^ The signatory. Creating the anchor is the buyer's act.
    seller : Party
      -- ^ An observer, so the counterparty can see and query the anchor it is a party to.
    atrHash : Text
      -- ^ 64-char lowercase hex, no `0x` prefix. Validated off-ledger by `atrHashToLedgerText`.
    paymentRef : Text
      -- ^ An opaque reference to the settlement this anchors. Empty when the deployment has none.
    createdAt : Text
      -- ^ ISO-8601 UTC, stamped by the creating client. Evidence of when, never used as a gate.
  where
    signatory buyer
    observer seller

    -- An anchor asserting an empty hash would be indistinguishable from an anchor asserting nothing, and
    -- a query filtered on `atrHash` would match it for every caller who passed an empty string.
    ensure atrHash /= ""

    -- Withdrawal is the buyer's alone, because the buyer is the signatory: archiving is undoing their own
    -- assertion. The seller keeps whatever it observed — Canton has no reversal, and an archived contract
    -- was still recorded in the transaction that created it.
    choice Withdraw : ()
      controller buyer
      do pure ()
